I want people in our audience to have an  understanding of the financial  indicators that are being discussed in  corporate media now that the stock  market is just crashing crashing  crashing everything's flying off the  walls virus oil Japan all of this  different stuff it's important to  understand what people are talking about  the entire yield curve for all federal  US bonds fell below 1% yesterday this is  the first time in history period that  this has ever happened what does this  mean and why do we care let's talk about  it first of all what are US bonds u.s.  bonds are debt securities issued by the  US Treasury so essentially the US  Treasury will borrow money from you if  you loan the government money by buying  a bond the government pays you an  interest rate that interest rate is what  we are talking about when we say the  bond yield there are different bond  durations the big ones that are the  leading indicators are the five year  bond the 10 year bond and the 30 year  bond and the idea is the longer term  bond pays a higher yield why well in  general if you were willing to lock your  money up for a longer period of time in  exchange for that you get a higher yield  a higher interest rate you make more  money for being willing to lock up your  money for a longer duration that's why a  5 year CD with your bank typically pays  more than a 1 year CD looking at the  crash in bond yields over the year so  far and including over the last few days  we see that all three of these bond  yields the 5 10 and 30-year fell below  1% yesterday to the area of 0.4 0.5  percent why is this happening and what  does it mean well with bonds yields and  prices move in opposite directions okay  so imagine you have a hundred dollar  bond that pays one percent when stocks  crash people want bonds because they are  seen as safer they are seen as a sure  bet where you will make something even  if it's only 1% so as people start  demanding more bonds the price of the  bonds in  someone might be willing to pay a  hundred and five dollars for a  hundred-dollar bond or a hundred and ten  dollars for a hundred-dollar bond that  1% interest rate calculated over a  hundred and five dollars or a hundred  and ten dollars is no longer a 1%  interest rate it's less so as people  demand more bonds the price of the bonds  goes up the yield is the same which  lowers the effective yield of the bond  the yield goes down since in uncertain  times people are willing to accept a  lower return because of the relative  safety of bonds over stocks so what we  are seeing here this crash and bond  prices is a rush to safety the virus  news the oil price is crashing the  Italian lock down the Japanese economic  contraction all this stuff makes people  think wow stocks are going to crash  which they are doing I'm going to move  my money to bonds this raises the price  of bonds lowering the yield the yield of  bonds and people are willing to accept  the lower yield if you expect stocks to  do well you wouldn't invest in bonds at  0.5% yield because you'd expect to make  way more money with stocks if you expect  stocks to lose money suddenly a sure bet  of making 0.5 percent on a u.s. bond  starts to look attractive this is a sign  an indicator of absolute and total panic  that the outlook is so negative that  bonds are dropping to these levels this  could be as bad as 2008 or maybe things  get under control in the next two months  and things don't get as bad as 2008 as I  told you earlier in the program I  already outlined what I am doing  financially it was not financial advice  but you can go back to our earlier  segments from today if that is  interesting to you one of our sponsors  is privacy calm they're giving you $5.00  when you sign up for their completely  free service at privacy calm slash  Pakman I've been using privacy for a  little over a year now you've heard me  talk about it before it's a life saver  and here's how it works takes just a  couple of 